## 2. The Handoff Protocol

### Why this matters

In a workflow (n8n, Zapier), agents are stateless API calls. Information is lost
between steps. The Assessment Agent doesn't know *why* this article was chosen.

In this system, the Content Agent writes a structured context object that the
Assessment Agent reads before doing anything. The agent has *intent*, not just data.

### The Pydantic model

```python
class ContentToAssessmentContext(BaseModel):
    article_id: UUID
    top_chunks: list[Chunk]           # pre-retrieved, relevance-scored against weak_concepts
    curation_rationale: str           # agent's plain-English reasoning
    suggested_focus: str              # e.g. "chunking strategy tradeoffs"
    user_context: UserContext

class UserContext(BaseModel):
    level: int                        # 1–5, from syllabus_state
    weak_concepts: list[str]          # topics with last_score < 0.6
    question_history: list[str]       # last 5 questions asked — avoid repeating
    session_time_budget_min: int      # from onboarding: 5 / 15 / 30
    answer_style_pref: str            # "conceptual" | "applied" — inferred from past answers

class Chunk(BaseModel):
    text: str
    relevance_score: float            # cosine similarity vs weak_concepts query
```

### Validation rule

If this model fails Pydantic validation, the session aborts at the handoff boundary —
not mid-quiz. The user sees a "retry" state, not a broken quiz.
The malformed payload is logged in full for debugging.

Fallback: Assessment Agent runs with article text only (no curation_rationale),
producing a less targeted but not broken quiz.

