## 5. Error Handling Strategy

### Principle

Silent failures are the real product risk. Every error must have:
1. A defined recovery path (user always gets something)
2. A log event with enough context to debug
3. No blank pages or broken quizzes visible to the user

### Error catalogue

| Scenario | Severity | Recovery |
|---|---|---|
| No qualifying articles found | P0 | Retry with relaxed threshold → static content bank fallback |
| LLM API timeout / 5xx | P0 | Exponential backoff (3x) → downgrade model → serve yesterday's digest |
| Pydantic validation fails on handoff | P1 | Abort at boundary → run assessment with article text only |
| Orchestrator exceeds max_steps=12 | P1 | Session marked incomplete → retry button shown → full state logged |
| Embedding storage fails | P2 | Continue without embedding → re-queue → dedup skipped for this article |
| Evaluator confidence persistently low | P2 | Switch to MCQ format for that topic until confidence stabilises |
| User scores 0.9+ for 5 sessions (plateau) | Handled | Auto-promote + topic rotation (not an error — a success signal) |
| Duplicate content slips through | P1 | Second cosine check before delivery → replace with next candidate |

### What NOT to do

- Never show a generic "something went wrong" page. Always tell the user what happened and give them an action.
- Never silently swallow an exception without logging the full payload.
- Never retry more than 3 times on LLM calls — you'll block the Celery worker queue.

